  "source_excerpts": {
    "title": "Residue-phase discrepancy certificates for the grumpy mean",
    "mechanism": "The representation is a union of modular lattice slices with multiplicities forgotten only after exact inclusion\u2013exclusion, paired with Sigma={increase a fixed round-robin horizon}. It is partial-action on the collision incidence arrangement, not point translation. The discarded labels are recovered only for overlap certificates; their number is charged.\n\nFor fixed M and horizon t, write the three pair-collision congruences in integer boxes, including modular wraps and division by 2. Decompose each intersection into floor-sum cells. Maintain upper and lower counts with an explicit boundary-cell budget B_t. Summing B_t/N over horizons bounds the error in E[T], rather than assuming that a pointwise limiting covered density may be integrated. The key test is whether sum_t B_t=O(N) uniformly in M=floor(mu sqrt(N)) and the residue phases; if only O(N log N) is available the normalized error is still O(log(N)/sqrt(N)), whereas a bulk O(N^{3/2}) discrepancy does not certify a limit. This audits the integration and limit interchange, not the already-known finite union identity.",
    "minimal_test": {
      "execution_status": "design_only",
      "design": "Design: freeze mu in {1/3,1/2,2/3}, N prime from 101 through 1009, every realizable phase (N mod M)/M. Compare floor-sum upper/lower counts with bitmaps at every horizon. Independently derive boundary envelopes symbolically and use the published finite-budget probability as a separate baseline fixture after matching schedules.",
      "controls": [
        {
          "kind": "matched_null",
          "design": "Integer interval unions with randomly shifted origins and the same lengths; they test floor/wrap counting without ECDLP structure."
        },
        {
          "kind": "known_false",
          "design": "Omit the wrap at N/2 in the 2Q collision family; the exact bitmap must exhibit a missing or doubly counted slope."
        },
        {
          "kind": "baseline",
          "design": "Reproduce the nearest record's relevant baseline and the declared public-input comparator with identical setup, query law, memory accounting and verification."
        }
      ],
      "metrics": [
        "certified normalized interval width and phase spread",
        "setup work",
        "field/group operations",
        "peak live words",
        "data and oracle queries",
        "useful output probability",
        "verification failures"
      ],
      "decision_threshold": "width <= C*log(N)/sqrt(N) with explicit C independent of phase; compare whether disjoint phase subsequences retain positive limiting separation",
      "stopping_rules": [
        "Stop at the first failed exact identity or known-false control and classify it as a procedure defect, not mathematical falsification.",
        "A future Executor must freeze finite instance/seed lists, wall-clock and memory caps before running; no run is authorized here.",
        "If prerequisite public inputs cannot be obtained within the separately frozen budget, report an infrastructure or interface limitation."
      ],
      "required_artifacts": [
        "frozen instance and parameter manifest",
        "exact baseline and null outputs",
        "per-instance metric and peak-memory trace",
        "independently checkable output certificates",
        "complete failures and exceptions"
      ],
      "budget": {
        "authorized_runs_in_this_task": 0,
        "future_experiment_status": "requires a new approved frozen handoff"
      }
    },
    "falsification_conditions": [
      "A ladder count outside the certified interval invalidates the certificate. A phase-dependent bulk discrepancy falsifies the uniform-constant route, leaving schedule-dependent constants open.",
      "A gain that survives the stated information-destroying null or known-false control without an explained structural distinction is an artifact.",
      "Only the exact family and threshold in minimal_test are in scope."
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      "The input is a public curve, a declared prime subgroup of order N, public base G and target Q unless the minimal test explicitly concerns an abstract module or torsor.",
      "No secret scalar labels, free orientation, free weak endpoint, uncharged dictionary or independent-sample oracle is available.",
      "The representation and its allowed operations are exactly the pair defined in mechanism; retained point information and branching are charged.",
      "A certified constant interval for a stated schedule and inversion convention."
    ],
